From aa5c08cd3bbb1be6ecd19bf10e3ceb20b8c38db5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Outcrop <outcrop@localhost>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 02:06:45 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] RAM tier: page-locked in segments of about 1 GiB, each
 before it is written (STRATA_PIN_SEGMENTS)

The paced path (#1250) took the arena's pages in 1 GiB steps and then page-locked them all in ONE registration. On
kernels that type pageblocks as CMA without a CMA area (Ubuntu's 7.0.0-42 and 7.0.0-43: CmaTotal 0, yet 2.8 GiB of
CMA-typed pageblocks on a 31 GiB machine), that registration can loop for many minutes: a long-term pin migrates the
pages it finds in those blocks and pins the whole range again, while compaction moves other pages into them. On a
Quadro M6000 machine with 31 GiB of RAM the 19.3 GiB tier took anywhere from 44 s to over 30 minutes to page-lock,
and during one evening's update check it stood at the page-lock for 131 s and 314 s.

Each step is now its own registration, made before anything is written into it. The pages a pin faults in itself are
allocated outside those blocks, so there is nothing to migrate, and a retry would redo one segment, not the arena.
Every cut is a page-aligned blob start: no page is registered twice and no blob spans two registrations. The
registered part is still a prefix, so pinned() and the copy sites are unchanged. When the segments' device aliases are
not one run (they were not, on that machine), device_alias() looks up the blob's own segment, and the opt-in exchange
rotation, which needs one run, stays off.

Measured there with the paced path forced (STRATA_PIN_GUARD=2) and compaction forced throughout the start
(/proc/sys/vm/compact_memory written in a loop): the single registration had not finished after 300 s, while the
segments locked 19.27 GiB in 20 segments in 22.1 s (the slowest 2.2 s). Decode speed and the answers are unchanged:
all 40 GSM8K answers came out the same, with the PCIe share reading RAM-tier experts through the segments' aliases.

STRATA_PIN_SEGMENTS=0 restores the single registration.
